namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

/*! 
\page rp_BinaryStructureFactor_page BinaryStructureFactor

This analyzer computes the structure factor for any "binary" system, 
with only two types of monomer. By default, it computes and outputs 
only the spherical average of the structure factor, for which each
value is an averaged over all wavevectors in a set (or "bunch") of
wavevectors of equal magnitude. This class can also optionally 
output structure factors for all individual wavevectors.

\see BinaryStructureFactor (class API)

\section rp_BinaryStructureFactor_formula_sec Mathematical Definitions

This class computes the structure factor \f$ S({\bf q}) \f$ for an 
incompressible system containing two monomer types, for all values
of the wavevector \f$ {\bf q}) \f$. Let the two monomer types be denoted
by subscripts 0 and 1. The structure factor computed here is defined 
as
\f[
  S(\mathbf{q}) 
  = \frac{1}{4V}\langle |\hat{C}_{-}({\bf q})|^{2} \rangle \quad,
\f]
where \f$ V \f$ is total system volume, and 
\f[
  \hat{C}_{-}({\bf q}) \equiv \int \! d{\bf r} 
                       \; e^{-i{\bf q}\cdot {\bf r}}
                       [ c_{0}({\bf r}) - c_{1}({\bf r}) ] 
  \quad.
\f]
Here, \f$ c_{i}({\bf r}) \f$ is the local concentration of monomers of 
type i, for i = 0 and i =1.

In a field-theoretic simulation, this is computed using the identity
\f[
  S(\mathbf{q}) = 
  \frac{1}{v^2\chi_{b}^2} \frac{1}{V}
  \left\langle |\hat{W}_{-}(\mathbf {q})|^{2} \right\rangle
   - \frac{1}{2v \chi_b} 
\f]
where \f$ v \f$ is monomer volume, \f$ \chi_{b} \f$ is the bare 
Flory-Huggins parameter, and
\f[
  \hat{W}_{-}({\bf q}) = \int \! d{\bf r} 
  \; e^{-i{\bf q}\cdot {\bf r}} 
  [ w_{0}({\bf r}) - w_{1}({\bf r})]/2 
\f]
is the transform of the exchange field 
\f$ W_{-}({\bf r}) = [ w_{0}({\bf r}) - w_{1}({\bf r})]/2 \f$.

<em> Technical note </em>:
The Fourier transform \f$ \hat{W}_{-}({\bf q}) \f$ that is defined above 
as an integral is approximated internally by the product of the system 
volume \f$ V \f$ and the normalized discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of 
\f$ W_{-}({\bf r}) \f$ that is computed by either 
Prdc::Cpu::FFT::forwardTransform or
Prdc::Cuda::FFT::forwardTransform .

\section rp_BinaryStructureFactor_parameter_sec Parameter File

The full parameter file format, including all optional parameters, 
is shown below:
\code
BinaryStructureFactor{
  interval           int
  outputFileName     string
  writeWaveData*     bool     (default false)
}
\endcode
Meanings of the parameters are described briefly below:
<table>
  <tr>
    <td> <b> Label </b>  </td>
    <td> <b> Description </b>  </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> interval </td>
    <td> number of steps between data samples </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
     <td> outputFileName </td>
     <td> name of output file </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
     <td> writeWaveData* </td>
     <td> should \f$ S({\bf q})\f$ be reported for individual waves? </td>
  </tr>
</table>
Note that the writeWaveData parameter is optional, and set to false (or 0) 
by default. 

\section rp_BinaryStructureFactor_output_sec Output Files
At the end of the simulation, this class outputs a file containing 
spherically-averaged values of \f$ S({\bf q}) \f$, averaged over time 
and over groups of wavevectors of the same magnitude. If writeWaveData 
is false (0. the default), then the name of this file is given by the 
outputFileName string parameter. If writeWaveData is set to true (1), 
then the name of this file is obtained by adding a suffix "_ave" to the 
end of the outputFileName string. In either case, the resulting file will 
contain two columns of numbers, in which the first column contains an 
ordered list of values of wavenumber \f$ q \f$, and the second column
contains a corresponding list of values of the spherically averaged 
structure factor \f$ S(q) \f$. 

If writeWaveData is true, the class also outputs a second file containing
values of S(q) for individual wavevectors. The name of this file is
obtained by adding a suffix "_wave" to the end of the outputFileName
string parameter.  The first D columns of this file contain integer 
indices for individual waves, using a standard DFT grid representation
in which all indices non-negative. These D columns are followed by a 
column containing the time averaged value of \f$ S({\bf q}) \f$ for 
each wavevector.

*/

}
}
